.\" The manual page: how to start tiny, its options, and its keys.
.\"
.\" From a checkout, `man ./docs/tiny.1` shows it. The installer puts it in
.\" share/man/man1 beside the binary's folder, where `man tiny` finds it.
.\"
.\" A test in src/main.rs reads this against the keymap and the options, so a
.\" key or an option that changes without changing here fails the build.
.TH TINY 1 2026-11-09 "tiny 0.7.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tiny \- an IDE, text editor, knowledge manager and file browser in the \%terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tiny
.br
.B tiny
.I file
.br
.B tiny
.I path
.br
.B tiny
.I option
.SH DESCRIPTION
This manual serves to be a quick guide on the controls.
Inside tiny,
.B ?
shows every key and command, with any you have changed.
.SH STARTING
.TP
.B tiny
Opens the folder you are in.
.TP
.BI tiny " file"
Edits
.IR file ,
across the whole window.
Esc brings the browser back, with the file's folder in it.
A name with an extension that does not exist yet is made as an empty file.
.TP
.BI tiny " path"
Opens the folder at
.IR path .
A folder that does not exist yet is made.
.PP
The installers also put tiny in the applications menu, the Start menu on
Windows and Launchpad on a Mac, where it opens in a terminal, and among the
programs a file manager offers for text files.
Choosing it there once, as the program for a kind of file, makes a
double-click open those in tiny.
Started from any of those, tiny opens with the browser hidden.
.PP
Inside tiny,
.B "*cd folder"
opens another folder as the project.
A name is a folder in the project,
.B ..
the one above it,
.B ~
home, and a full path goes there.
.B *cd
alone goes home, and
.B "*cd -"
back to the project before.
Open files stay open, with their changes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Shows how to start tiny.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Shows the version.
.TP
.B \-\-hide\-browser
Starts with the browser hidden: the file across the whole window, or the
folder.
Ctrl+T shows the browser.
It is how the applications menu starts tiny.
.TP
.B \-\-update
Installs the newest release, if there is one.
A tiny your package manager installed, from the AUR say, is left for it to
update.
.TP
.B \-\-config
Prints where the config file is.
.TP
.B \-\-licenses
Prints the licenses of everything built into tiny.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall
Removes tiny, its config file and its place in the applications menu.
Your own files are not touched.
A tiny your package manager installed is left for it to remove.
.SH CONTROLS
.SS Picking a part
The window has parts: the browser, each pane, and the terminal.
The keyboard is either in one of them, which a green outline says, or out
of them all, picking one, which a white outline says.
.TP
.B "Up, Down, Left, Right, i, k, j, l"
Pick the part that way.
Toward the browser, while it is hidden, it comes back.
.TP
.B Enter
Go into the part that is picked.
.TP
.B Esc
Out of a part, to pick another.
Esc never quits; Ctrl+Q does.
.SS Anywhere
These work from every pane, the file you are typing in included.
In a file,
.B /
and
.B ?
are typed like any other character, so use Ctrl+F and F1 there.
.TP
.B Ctrl+S
Save. With a folder selected in the browser, save every file in it.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Q
Quit. tiny asks first if anything is unsaved.
.TP
.B "Ctrl+F, /"
Search the project.
Between slashes, like
.BR "/fn \(rsw+/" ,
the search is a regular expression.
Type
.B *
first for a command, like
.BR "*goto main" ,
where Tab finishes the word being typed: a verb, a name, a setting or its
value, and Tab again gives the next.
.TP
.B "F1, ?"
Every key and command.
.TP
.B *
The bar, with a command begun.
.TP
.B Ctrl+G
The git window, and back.
.TP
.B Ctrl+L
The map, and back.
.TP
.B Ctrl+T
Hide or show the browser.
.TP
.B Ctrl+N
Another tiny on the same folder, in a new window.
.TP
.B Ctrl+W
The next pane.
With one, it splits in two.
.B *split
and
.B "*split down"
add a pane beside or below the one you are in, and
.B *close
closes it.
.TP
.B Ctrl+O
The terminal, and back.
It runs your shell in the project's folder, under the panes, and every key
but Ctrl+O goes to it, and Esc at the shell's prompt, which steps out.
A program that takes the whole screen gets Esc as well.
.B exit
closes it.
.SS The browser
.TP
.B "Up, i"
Up a row.
.TP
.B "Down, k"
Down a row.
.TP
.B "Right, l"
Open a folder, or step into an open one.
.TP
.B "Left, j"
Close a folder, or go to the one it is in.
.TP
.B Enter
Edit a file, or open or close a folder.
Pictures, videos, PDFs and sounds open in your own viewer.
.TP
.B "Home, Shift+I"
The first row.
.TP
.B "End, Shift+K"
The last row.
.TP
.B "Page Up, Ctrl+U"
A screen up.
.TP
.B "Page Down, Ctrl+D"
A screen down.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Copy the file or folder under the cursor.
.TP
.B Ctrl+V
Paste it into the folder the cursor is in.
In Windows Terminal, which keeps Ctrl+V for itself, this is Ctrl+P.
.TP
.B .
Show or hide hidden files.
.TP
.B "<, >"
Narrower or wider.
.TP
.B Esc
Out of the browser, to pick another part.
.SS Editing a file
Letters, Enter, Tab, Backspace and Delete type as they do anywhere.
Enter keeps the indent.
Shift with any key that moves the cursor selects as it moves.
.TP
.B "Up, Down, Left, Right"
Move the cursor.
.TP
.B "Ctrl+Left, Ctrl+Right, Alt+Left, Alt+Right"
A word at a time.
.TP
.B Home
The start of the line: the indent first, then the margin.
.TP
.B "End, Ctrl+E"
The end of the line.
.TP
.B "Page Up, Ctrl+U"
A screen up.
.TP
.B "Page Down, Ctrl+D"
A screen down.
.TP
.B "Ctrl+Home, Ctrl+End"
The first and the last line.
.TP
.B "Tab, Shift+Tab"
At the end of a word, Tab finishes it with a longer word from the file,
the nearest first, and Tab again puts in the next.
Anywhere else it puts spaces up to the next tab stop, or with lines
selected, indents them.
Shift+Tab takes the indent off.
.TP
.B Ctrl+A
Select everything.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Copy what is selected, or with nothing selected, the line.
.TP
.B Ctrl+X
Cut what is selected, or the line.
.TP
.B Ctrl+V
Paste.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Z
Undo.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Y
Redo.
.TP
.B Ctrl+K
Delete the line.
.TP
.B Alt+N
Select the word under the cursor, then each next place it is, with a
cursor of its own.
Whatever is typed then goes in at every cursor.
.TP
.B "Alt+Shift+Up, Alt+Shift+Down"
Another cursor on the line above or below.
On a Mac these and Alt+N need Option set to send Meta, in the terminal's
settings.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R
Start recording keys, or stop.
.TP
.B Ctrl+P
Play back what was recorded.
.B "*play 20"
plays it twenty times.
.TP
.B Esc
Let go of the selection and the other cursors, or with none, out of the
file, to pick another part.
.SS The mouse
A click puts the cursor where it lands, and a drag selects.
A double click selects a word, a triple click a line, and a click on the
line numbers a line too.
Alt and a click, or Ctrl and a click, adds a cursor.
Shift and a click selects to there, where the terminal lets Shift through:
most keep Shift and a drag for selecting their own text, which still copies
anything on the screen.
.PP
In a list, the first click picks and the second opens: a row of the browser,
a box on the map, a change or a button in the git window.
A search result opens with one.
A click goes into the part it lands on, the terminal included.
A drag on the border between two parts resizes them: the browser's edge,
the border between two panes or above the terminal, and in the git window
the list's edge and the border between the two halves of a diff.
Neither part is made smaller than leaves room for what it holds.
The wheel scrolls whatever it is over, whichever part the keyboard is in.
A program in the terminal that asks for the mouse gets it there.
.PP
A click on a link follows it: a web address opens in your browser, and a
link to a file, or a wikilink, opens that file in tiny.
In text being edited a click places the cursor, so there it is Ctrl and a
click.
.SS The map
.TP
.B "Up, Down, Left, Right, i, k, j, l"
Move to the nearest file that way.
.TP
.B "Tab, Shift+Tab"
The next and the previous file.
.TP
.B Enter
Edit the file.
.TP
.B "1, 2, 3, 4"
Show or hide wikilinks, links, calls and references.
.TP
.B r
Read the project again.
.TP
.B Esc
Back to the browser.
.SS The git window
.TP
.B "Up, Down, i, k"
Move through the changes.
.TP
.B "Home, End, Shift+I, Shift+K"
The first and the last row.
.TP
.B "Left, Right, j, l"
Move between the buttons.
.TP
.B Enter
Stage or unstage a file, or a whole list from its heading, or press a button.
In the branch list, switch to the branch, or make one.
.TP
.B n
A new branch, from the one checked out.
.TP
.B d
Delete the branch under the cursor, asking first.
.TP
.B Shift+R
Rename the branch under the cursor.
.TP
.B m
Merge the branch under the cursor into the one checked out, asking first.
.TP
.B Tab
Edit the file.
.TP
.B "Page Up, Page Down, Ctrl+U, Ctrl+D"
Scroll the diff.
.TP
.B r
Ask git again.
.TP
.B "<, >"
Narrower or wider.
.TP
.B Esc
Out of the list or the diff, to pick the other with the arrows; again, back
to the browser.
.PP
Under the changes are the branches: the ones here, with a
.B *
on the one checked out, and the ones on a remote that no branch here
follows yet, which Enter makes here.
Switching and merging wait until every file is saved.
The same are
.BR "*branch <name>" ,
.BR "*switch <name>" ,
.BR "*delete branch <name>" ,
.B "*rename branch <name> to <new>"
and
.BR "*merge <name>" .
.PP
In a project that is not a git repository yet, the window offers to
initialize one, to publish the project, or to clone a repository into it.
The same are
.BR *init ,
.B *publish
and
.BR "*clone <address>" .
Publishing makes the repository if there is none, commits everything as the
first commit if nothing has been committed, and pushes it: to the remote it
has, to an address typed after the word, or to a new private GitHub
repository made with the gh program.
.B "*publish public"
makes it public.
.PP
The browser colors what git says about each file: yellow for changed since
the last commit, green for new, gray for what .gitignore leaves out, and red
for a conflict.
A folder takes the color of what is inside it.
.PP
Beside the line numbers of a file, a line new since the last commit is
marked green and a changed one blue, and a red mark at the foot of a line
says lines after it were deleted.
The marks follow the file as it is typed, saved or not, and go once it is
committed.
.B "*set line_marks false"
turns them off.
